          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A paver in September sun can be 20 degrees hotter than the air around it. That gap is why so many people seal on a pleasant afternoon and get a blotchy, cloudy finish they cannot explain.
        </p>

        <div id="the-window" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Window
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most paver sealers specify an application range somewhere around 10 to 32 degrees Celsius, or 50 to 90 Fahrenheit. The practical window is narrower than the specified one, because the specification describes what the product tolerates rather than what produces the best result.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface Temperature</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Verdict</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Below 7°C / 45°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Do not seal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Film may never coalesce properly</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">7-10°C / 45-50°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Marginal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slow cure, weak film risk</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">10-16°C / 50-60°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acceptable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slow but sound if dry</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">16-27°C / 60-80°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ideal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good flow, reliable cure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">27-32°C / 80-90°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Marginal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flashes fast, lap marks likely</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Above 32°C / 90°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Do not seal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Carrier flashes before it penetrates</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The ideal band is 16 to 27 degrees, and within it the lower half is generally better than the upper, because the product has time to flow out and level before the carrier leaves. A mild, overcast day at 18 degrees is close to perfect conditions.
          </p>

          <p>
            The forecast night temperature matters as much as the day. A water-based film applied at 20 degrees in the afternoon that then sits through a 5-degree night has not finished coalescing when the temperature drops below the threshold, and the result is a weak, chalky film that fails early. Check the overnight low before you open the tin, not just the afternoon high.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TWO CHECKS PEOPLE SKIP</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Measure the paver, and check tonight's low.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Air temperature and surface temperature diverge enormously in sun. And a film applied in a warm afternoon still has to cure through the night, so a cold night undoes a warm day. Both checks take a minute and both are routinely omitted.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="surface-temp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Surface Temperature, Not Air Temperature
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the distinction that explains most unexplained failures on days that seemed fine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete and stone absorb solar radiation and hold it. A paver in direct sun can sit 15 to 25 degrees Celsius above the surrounding air, so a pleasant 24-degree afternoon can present a 45-degree surface. Dark pavers run hotter than light ones, and a driveway that has baked since morning is hotter than a patio that came out of shade an hour ago.
          </p>

          <p>
            At that surface temperature the carrier flashes off almost on contact. A water-based sealer loses its water before the acrylic particles can coalesce, leaving a powdery, weak film. A solvent-based one skins instantly, trapping solvent underneath, which is a direct route to blushing.
          </p>

          <p>
            An infrared thermometer costs very little and removes the guesswork entirely. Point it at the pavers in several places — the sunny middle, the shaded edge, the section that has been in sun longest — and work from those numbers rather than from the forecast. It is the single most useful ten-dollar tool for this job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Without one, the reliable approach is simply to keep the surface out of direct sun. Work in the morning on a west-facing patio and the afternoon on an east-facing one, or follow the shade around, or pick an overcast day. Overcast is genuinely the best sealing weather and people avoid it because it feels like poor weather.
          </p>

          <p>
            The back of your hand is a crude but workable check: if the paver feels warm rather than neutral, it is too warm. If you cannot hold your palm flat on it comfortably, it is far too warm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-cold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Too Cold: The Failure That Shows Up Later
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cold failures are insidious because the patio often looks acceptable on the day and fails a season later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based sealers depend on coalescence: the suspended acrylic particles must flow together and fuse into a continuous film as the water leaves. That process has a minimum film formation temperature, and below it the particles simply never fuse. What you get is a film that looks roughly right and has the mechanical integrity of chalk — it powders, it fails to protect, and it wears through within a season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solvent-based sealers are more tolerant at the low end because the resin is dissolved rather than suspended, so there is no coalescence step to fail. They still cure slowly in cold and remain very intolerant of moisture, and cold pavers are frequently damp pavers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Frost is an absolute stop. Sealing when frost is possible within the curing window risks water freezing in the surface and in the film, which destroys adhesion. In practice that means stopping well before the first frosts rather than on the day one arrives.
          </p>

          <p>
            The common late-season trap is a run of mild days in autumn with cold nights. The afternoon reads 16 degrees and feels ideal; the night drops to 4. The film has not finished forming when it crosses below the threshold. If the overnight low is below about 10 degrees, wait for a better week or accept that the result may be compromised.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold pavers also hold moisture longer. A patio that had rain three days ago is genuinely dry in July and often still damp in October, which compounds the problem. Our guide on how to seal pavers covers the dryness checks that matter more in cool weather.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-hot" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Too Hot: The Failure You See Immediately
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heat failures are at least obvious, which is a small mercy.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a hot surface the carrier leaves faster than the sealer can flow out and penetrate. The result is a film that sits on top rather than bonding into the surface, with visible lap marks where each pass began to set before the next overlapped it, and often a blotchy appearance where absorption varied.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lap marks are the signature heat failure. Because the working edge sets before you return to it, each overlap shows as a line or a band of different sheen, and once cured it does not blend out. On a large patio in sun this can appear within minutes of starting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solvent-based products in heat also risk blushing, because the surface skins over and traps solvent below. That produces the same milky white result as trapped moisture, from the opposite cause.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fixes are all scheduling rather than technique. Work early morning or late afternoon, follow the shade, choose an overcast day, or wait for a milder week. Trying to outrun a hot surface by working faster does not succeed; the surface sets faster than any person can roll.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical note: do not wet the pavers to cool them down. You will solve the temperature problem and create a moisture problem, which is worse and harder to see. If they are too hot, the answer is to come back later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="humidity-rain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Humidity, Rain and the Clear Window
          </h2>

          <p>
            Temperature is the headline variable and moisture is the one that actually ruins more patios.
          </p>

          <p>
            High humidity slows evaporation, which extends every stage of drying and curing. On a still, humid day a water-based sealer can take twice as long to reach foot traffic, and the extended vulnerable window means more opportunity for dew, a shower or an errant sprinkler to land on it. Aim for below about 70 percent relative humidity if you can choose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rain is the outright stop. You want a genuinely clear window — most products want at least 24 hours, some more — and that window includes the night, when dew forms on a cooling surface. A forecast of dry afternoon followed by an overnight shower is not a sealing day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dew deserves specific attention because it catches people out. On a clear, calm night a paver surface can cool below the dew point and collect moisture even with no rain at all, which on a film that has not cured produces spotting and blushing. Sealing early in the day so the film has maximum time before nightfall reduces the risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is the moisture already in the pavers. They must be dry through, not just on the surface, which after washing means 24 to 48 hours of dry weather and considerably longer for a shaded patio. Confirm with the taped plastic square test rather than assuming.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Conditions Checklist</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">16-27°C</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ideal surface temperature</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Under 70%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Relative humidity</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Clear window needed, minimum</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Above 10°C</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Overnight low</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="season" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Picking the Season
          </h2>

          <p>
            Given the constraints, some parts of the year are much better than others, and it is worth planning rather than seizing a free weekend.
          </p>

          <p>
            Late spring and early autumn are generally ideal in temperate climates. Mild days, mild nights, moderate sun angle so surfaces do not bake, and typically a settled spell available. Late May and early September are the classic windows.
          </p>

          <p>
            High summer is workable but demands scheduling around the sun. In many places the only viable hours are early morning, and a large patio may need two sessions. Surface temperature is the binding constraint rather than air temperature.
          </p>

          <p>
            Late autumn is the trap discussed above: pleasant afternoons, cold nights, damp pavers that never quite dry, and shortening days that leave less curing time before dark. If you have left it late, the honest advice is usually to wait for spring.
          </p>

          <p>
            Winter is out in any climate with frost. In genuinely mild winter climates it can be workable, but damp is usually the limiting factor rather than cold.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a sequencing consideration. Newly laid pavers need 60 to 90 days to weather and release efflorescence before sealing, so a patio laid in August is a spring sealing job regardless of the autumn weather. Our guide on whether pavers need sealing covers that waiting period.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if you are stripping an old sealer first, allow for the extra drying time afterwards. Stripping, rinsing and washing leaves pavers holding a lot of water, so the schedule is strip one weekend, dry through the week, seal the next — which needs two good weekends in a row.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. A Go or No-Go Checklist
          </h2>

          <p>
            Run through these on the morning. If any answer is no, do not open the tin.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Check</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Requirement</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surface temperature</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-27°C, ideally 16-21°C</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Direct sun on the surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Overnight low tonight</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Above 10°C</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rain forecast next 24 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Relative humidity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Below about 70%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pavers dry through</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-48 hr since any wetting</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Joint sand in and cured</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Irrigation switched off</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, for 3 days</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The last two are not temperature questions and belong on the list anyway, because they are the other two things that ruin a technically well-timed application. Polymeric sand needs curing before sealing, and a sprinkler running the morning after undoes everything.
          </p>

          <p>
            If conditions are marginal rather than wrong, the sensible response is to reduce the risk rather than proceed as normal. Apply thinner coats, work smaller sections, start earlier in the day, and accept that the job may take longer. A marginal day handled carefully beats a good day handled carelessly.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if the window genuinely is not there, wait. An unsealed patio is fine indefinitely — pavers are engineered to work unsealed, as our guide on whether they need sealing explains — whereas a badly cured film is a stripping job. There is no deadline here that justifies sealing in poor conditions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy the infrared thermometer. It removes the single largest source of error in this whole subject, it costs about as much as a gallon of sealer, and it will tell you in ten seconds what a forecast cannot tell you at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Air Temperature Instead of Surface Temperature</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A paver in direct sun can sit 15 to 25 degrees above the surrounding air, so a pleasant 24-degree afternoon can present a 45-degree surface where the carrier flashes off on contact. Point an infrared thermometer at the pavers, or simply keep the surface out of direct sun.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Overnight Low</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A water-based film applied at a comfortable 20 degrees still has to finish coalescing through the night, and a drop to 5 degrees stops that process partway. The result is a chalky, weak film that looks acceptable and fails within a season. Check tonight's low, not just today's high.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing in Late Autumn Because the Afternoon Feels Mild</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mild days with cold nights, pavers that never dry properly, and shortening daylight all stack against you. This is the classic trap that produces early failures. If you have left it late in the season, waiting for spring is usually the better decision.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Wetting Hot Pavers to Cool Them Down</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This trades a visible temperature problem for an invisible moisture one, and trapped moisture under a film is the harder of the two to fix. If the surface is too hot, the only answer is to come back at a cooler time of day or on a cooler day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a Dry Afternoon as a Clear Window</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most products need at least 24 hours without rain, and that window includes the night, when a cooling surface can collect dew even with no rain forecast. A dry afternoon followed by an overnight shower is not a sealing day.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best temperature to seal pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A surface temperature between 16 and 27 degrees Celsius, ideally in the lower half of that band, out of direct sun, with an overnight low above 10 degrees and no rain for at least 24 hours. A mild overcast day at around 18 degrees is close to perfect conditions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal pavers in cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Below about 10 degrees Celsius a water-based sealer may not coalesce properly, producing a chalky film with the integrity of powder that fails within a season. Solvent-based is more tolerant at the low end but cures slowly and is very intolerant of the damp that cold pavers usually hold. Below 7 degrees, do not seal.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can it be too hot to seal pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is more common than people expect because surface temperature runs far above air temperature in sun. On a hot surface the carrier flashes before the sealer can flow out and penetrate, producing lap marks, blotchy absorption and, with solvent products, blushing from trapped solvent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does humidity affect sealing pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Considerably. High humidity slows evaporation, which extends every stage of drying and curing and widens the window in which dew, a shower or a sprinkler can damage the film. Aim for below about 70 percent relative humidity, and be aware that a still humid day can double the wait before foot traffic.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I measure paver surface temperature?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An infrared thermometer, which costs roughly the same as a gallon of sealer and removes the largest source of error in this subject. Point it at several places — sunny middle, shaded edge, the area that has been in sun longest. Without one, keep the surface out of direct sun entirely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What time of day is best to seal pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Early morning is usually best, because surfaces are coolest and the film gets maximum daylight hours to cure before nightfall and dew. Late afternoon works on a cool day but leaves less curing time before the temperature drops. On an overcast day the timing matters much less.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best time of year to seal pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Late spring and early autumn in temperate climates, when days and nights are both mild and the sun angle is low enough that surfaces do not bake. High summer works if you schedule around the sun. Late autumn is a trap — mild afternoons, cold nights, damp pavers and short days.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

