        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Aim for a surface temperature of 16 to 27 degrees Celsius, out of direct sun, humidity below about 70 percent, a clear 24 hours ahead and an overnight low above 10. A mild overcast day is the best sealing weather there is, and people pass it up because it does not feel like good weather.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The two checks almost everyone skips are measuring the paver rather than reading the forecast, and looking at tonight's low rather than today's high. A surface in sun runs 15 to 25 degrees hotter than the air, and a film applied on a warm afternoon still has to finish curing through a cold night. An infrared thermometer costs about the same as a gallon of sealer and removes the larger of those two errors entirely.
          </p>
